  "result": "The 60-minute lesson on O3 is written at `lessons/0001-context-and-memory-in-an-agent.html`. I couldn't run a word count, so I haven't confirmed it falls in the length range for 60 minutes. My section-by-section estimate puts it at roughly 6,000 to 7,000 words against a fit range of 5,400 to 13,500.\n\n- **Context window:** what it holds and why every call resends everything. Costs follow a worked example (17,800 input tokens for one student question) and the quadratic cost formula.\n- **Between steps:** the message history as a Python list your program owns. When it grows too large, you can clear old tool results, summarize, or write notes outside the context.\n- **Between sessions:** nothing persists unless the program saves it. Exact facts such as training status go in a database; fuzzy observations go in short notes.\n- **Retrieval:** chunking, keyword and embedding indexes, and a cosine-similarity ranking worked by hand. It also covers why retrieval fails and `search_manuals` as a tool.\n- **Practice:** a placement exercise for the makerspace assistant and a token-cost exercise, plus five misconception boxes and three figures. The checks use immediate feedback.\n\nSources are three Anthropic engineering pages. I read search-result excerpts and summaries, not the full pages. The retrieval failure rates (5.7%, 3.7%, 2.9%) are Anthropic's own measurements, not independent ones, and I flagged them as such in the lesson. I also wrote `RESOURCES.md` and updated `NOTES.md`.\n\nQuiz 0002 (five items) and its key are ready for when you want them. Ask me anything about the lesson.\n\nOne question for you: do you want a one-page cheat sheet on context and memory decisions? I skipped it this session.",
